# TEMPLATE — do not edit the rendered patches/llm-egress-cluster-cidrs.yaml (gitignored).
# deploy/scripts/minikube-detect-cluster-cidrs.sh renders this file, replacing
# __CLUSTER_INTERNAL_CIDRS__ with the live cluster's pod + Service CIDRs and
# __CLUSTER_NODE_CIDRS__ with the node InternalIPs + apiserver endpoint (/32).
# Committing real CIDRs here would dirty every contributor's tree and bake one
# environment's pod/service CIDRs into a shared manifest — they differ between a
# dev minikube and prod (distinct pod/Service CIDRs, apiserver reachability).
#
# WHAT THIS ENABLES — the cluster-internal guard on the egress broker. HCC's
# openaiEgressBrokerReconciler rejects an operator baseURL that points at a
# cluster-internal address so a local-model Host cannot be turned into an SSRF
# pivot into the control plane. The guard combines two things:
#
#   * The runtime `/32` egress NetworkPolicy HCC emits pins the ONE LAN
#     destination the operator chose. The `/32` does not vouch for that
#     destination — it only guarantees the broker can reach nothing else
#     outbound; whether the chosen IP is safe is what the CIDR check below
#     decides.
#   * CONTEXT_MAPPER_CLUSTER_INTERNAL_CIDRS (pod + Service ranges) lets HCC
#     reject a baseURL whose IP falls inside cluster space — an apiserver/pod
#     ClusterIP — which CEL in the CRD cannot compute (no CIDR arithmetic).
#     HCC is FAIL-CLOSED on this value: without it, it refuses to provision any
#     broker at all (CONTEXT_MAPPER_OAI_EGRESS_REQUIRE_CLUSTER_CIDRS=true, the
#     base default). That is why this patch is rendered and applied BY DEFAULT.
#   * CONTEXT_MAPPER_CLUSTER_NODE_CIDRS (node + control-plane ranges) closes the
#     gap the pod+Service ranges leave open: the node IP and the apiserver
#     endpoint. A minikube node (e.g. 192.168.49.2) is RFC1918, so without this
#     HCC would read https://192.168.49.2:10250/ (kubelet) as a legitimate LAN
#     target. HCC is FAIL-CLOSED on this value too (same opt-out knob exempts
#     both categories). In minikube the script fills it from the node
#     InternalIPs + apiserver endpoint; in a managed cluster (GKE, ...) declare
#     it explicitly — the node-pool subnet plus masterIpv4CidrBlock.
#
# This patch is listed in the overlay's patchesStrategicMerge and rendered on
# every `make minikube-deploy-all`; the rendered file is gitignored, only this
# template is tracked. A bare `kubectl apply -k` without first running
# `make minikube-detect-cluster-cidrs` will fail on the missing rendered file —
# run the script (or make target) first.
#
# The apiserver-reachable ranges (CONTEXT_MAPPER_K8S_API_CIDRS) stay OPT-IN and
# are NOT set here: they also drive the `allow-k8s-api-egress-*` NetworkPolicies
# and the watch-recovery E2E fixtures, so turning them on is a separate,
# deliberate step (see docs/deploy/llm-providers.md).
#
# CIDR FORMAT — comma-separated. CONTEXT_MAPPER_CLUSTER_INTERNAL_CIDRS is the
# pod + Service CIDRs; CONTEXT_MAPPER_CLUSTER_NODE_CIDRS is the node + apiserver
# ranges (node IPs / endpoint as /32, plus node-pool subnets). Both are passed
# as-is to the runtime LAN classifier (no over-broad rejection), so set them
# tightly — an over-broad range like 10.0.0.0/8 would swallow legitimate RFC1918
# LAN targets as cluster-internal.
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: host-context-controller
  namespace: control-plane
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: host-context-controller
          env:
            - name: CONTEXT_MAPPER_CLUSTER_INTERNAL_CIDRS
              value: "__CLUSTER_INTERNAL_CIDRS__"
            - name: CONTEXT_MAPPER_CLUSTER_NODE_CIDRS
              value: "__CLUSTER_NODE_CIDRS__"
